From 9576dd80192af42e0658b009affa538225b7902d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: halt95 <halt95@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 17 Sep 2026 02:41:21 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 71/76] v2-rc6: instrumentation readers never wait on the
 compute stream (fixes qwen38next-flash#17)

Root cause (py-spy dumps, 2026-09-17 02:28 UTC, block 5 r5 on rc5; same signature as the
V.5 run 3 hang on rc4): the rc3 guard-telemetry cadence reader called buf.tolist() every
50 ms from its thread - a synchronous pageable device-to-host copy on the compute stream.
The CUDA driver services such a copy holding the context lock until the stream drains.
The compute stream held the PLE pull transport GPU-side wait; its producer needs the
same lock (the connector thread event.synchronize() and the main thread next
cuLaunchKernel, both found blocked on the lock). Circular wait; the executor timeout
(300 s) then killed the engine. The QSA clip reader (.item() per interval) shares the
pattern.

Fix: NonBlockingReader - per registered counter tensor a pinned host mirror, a dedicated
side CUDA stream, a non-blocking copy and event.query() polling; nothing in the reader
waits on the GPU. Registration happens in prealloc() before capture or lazily from the
reader thread (never from an allocating thread that may be capturing; register() raises
under is_current_stream_capturing). A count rise seen in one copy is reported from the
next (the kernels write the count before the maxima; the copy is not a coherent
snapshot). The shutdown drain reads through the mirror after the device synchronize.
Both readers use it.

Tests: tests/v1/worker/gpu/test_guard_reader_nonblocking.py - a reader tick must return
< 0.25 s while torch.cuda._sleep is parked on the compute stream (main-thread and
reader-thread variants; the pre-fix reader waits for the kernel), capture refusal,
confirming-tick reporting, no-loss after a stall.

Luna rounds 1-2 applied (capture-safe registration, confirming tick, drain via mirror,
QSA registration moved to the reader thread).
